WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.000
14 years after you fell in love with The Incredibles,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.240
your favorite super family returns with an all-new adventure

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.720
featuring surprise twists that will keep you guessing.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.440
Unless you remember anything at all about the first movie.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.480
"You can't count on anyone, especially your heroes."

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.500
"Superheroes keep us weak!"

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.140
Eh, it's a safe bet, actually.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.760
Pixar's target demo was Jack-Jack's age back then.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.760
Experience fan-favorite director Brad Bird's sixth feature film

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.700
and his fourth about how regular schmucks like us need to suck it up and let the gifted play by their own rules.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.700
In a Pixar film that continues their tradition of passable sequels that don't hit the mark of the first one,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.320
except that one time when it was even better

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.020
and that one time when it was so, so much worse.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.800
"Agent? You mean like insurance agent? Like..."

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.440
♫ Like a good neighbor, Mater is- ♫

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.260
NO!

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.060
Suit up once more with the Incredibles.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.520
Bob is a dad who just wants to turn his children into living weapons

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.380
while Helen just wants a cool action setpiece of her own for once.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.160
Last time, they learned to trust each other and fight crime as a family unit

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:23.340
after a mishap where one of the parents did illegal hero work on behalf of a techno genius who turned out to be a villain

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.820
and the public learned to love superheroes again.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.360
Now, after 15 minutes of erasing all that progress,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.380
"So now, we've gotta go back to never using our powers."

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.300
watch the Parrs learn to trust each other again

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.240
and fight crime as a family unit...

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.180
again.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.840
After a mishap where one of the parents does illegal hero work again on behalf of a techno-genius again

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.960
who turns out to be a villain...again.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.780
And the public learns to love superheroes again again.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.080
And if you think they're gonna mess with this formula for the next one,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.840
I can give you 1.2 billion reasons why they won't.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.580
Looking to spoil the fun is the evil Screenslaver,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.920
a villain who uses screens to hurt people,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.480
including the audience.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.820
She believes heroes are a dangerous menace we shouldn't rely on,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.260
which the movie never really disputes,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.260
"That's a rocket launcher!"

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.020
"Sweet!"

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.300
and has some incredibly solid points about the harm of society's nonstop consumption of media,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.040
"Break a sweat, participate in life."

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.580
which of course makes her the villain in a Disney movie!

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.200
Shut up, evil lady that wants us to go outside!

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.360
Real heroes are already subscribed to Disney's streaming service!

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.180
Woohoo! Look at all that IP!

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.860
Aw, yeah. Get me all up in all that IP, man! I wanna bathe in it.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.940
So enjoy a film with the exact same dynamic as the first time around,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.720
that's still incredibly charming,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.660
and holy moly, does it look good.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.980
But after 20 films,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.320
if another Pixar movie has a chase scene for a climax,

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:45.980
I'm gonna Dash myself off a cliff.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.440
"We're almost there!"

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.720
"I'm gonna be sick, I'm gonna be sick."

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.060
Stop chasing!

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:53.820
There's other ways to end a film, you know!

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:59.940
Why don't you switch things up with a huge, world-ending threat that, like, menaces people by projecting some kind of, like, sky beam or something?

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:00.560
Wait.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.520
What am I saying?

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.140
I've become what I hate the most!

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.000
Never mind!

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.840
Chase away to infinity and beyond!

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.180
Catherine Keener just really wanted to play another hypnotist?

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.920
Run, Frozone, run!

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.720
"You...have...POWERS!"

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.560
"Jack-Jack has powers?!"

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.880
Yeah, Incredibles, everyone knows Jack-Jack has powers. We found out in the last movie.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.340
And Rick Dicker found out from the babysitter in Jack-Jack Attack.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.400
Kinda surprised he didn't clue you in on all of this.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.860
That's a real Rick Dicker move.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.440
Heh heh, Rick Dicker.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.520
That's a funny name.

